f 1039 "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208847 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186208289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208847 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2198cf08-3a99-5e5d-80af-bbe722b51647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186208929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186208930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186552433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186552476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Chain Rule to Gradients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186208272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208848 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0c77271f-3d21-5193-a1ad-5c0264e337f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186209875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Vector Gradients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186209870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208872 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e03cea40-d542-50a2-beef-0257307c0a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186205482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186205483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186205484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-275963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aab571cd-61da-5cbe-9484-d00335e3954d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186208956 #edb/ref 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-2 \\\\ 2-2 \\\\ 2-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186209871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209870 #edb/ref 1061 610963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b43433a0-9dff-53f0-b8cf-64336b4b2fc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186209878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209871 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186209872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209871 #edb/ref 1061 610964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209871 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7685ad5-7aa7-532e-936b-bd0ad89e40bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209871 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186209954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209872 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186209873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209872 #edb/ref 1061 610965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209872 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1f3213b1-5ea3-5cd6-9b86-84bd1ef095b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209872 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186208846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209873 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186209874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209873 #edb/ref 1061 610968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209873 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc662f29-b540-527e-bb28-752fc9820d44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209873 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186208847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209874 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186209875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209874 #edb/ref 1061 610971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42b51395-66f8-5f41-b3b6-f3e548175320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186209877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209875 #edb/ref 1061 610972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209875 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a7727ac-4542-5d23-af4c-7a22c6bc3047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209875 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186208848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209877 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot {{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209877 #edb/ref 1065 12301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209877 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "eb90df7d-906c-5d50-a61a-40222511f26e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209877 #edb/ref 1067 "Computing Gradients With the Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209878 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x,$ let's first write\n![](images/39/394b4241f76616fae493bcc281cbe782c1d1a89680ef24cd2776c02302b9550d.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & ⋯ & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1} & {a}_{n2} & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}{x}_{1}+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{x}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{21}{x}_{1}+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{2n}{x}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1}{x}_{1}+{a}_{n2}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{nn}{x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{x}_{j} \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{2j}{x}_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{nj}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n![](images/15/15180b9898708fa3f2398a534c0e4f0a57f4466abd046b388eb7af3009e534b0.svg)\n\nEach term of this product looks like $x_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j$, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209878 #edb/ref 1065 12386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209878 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "46d7287c-3842-57d9-8f8b-8f5f95c5362f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209878 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209954 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f.$\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k.$ First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) =  \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j \n+ \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k \n+ \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j \n+ A_{kk} x_k^2.\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$. \n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k,$\n\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k,$ and\n\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k.$\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{k}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}[{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}+{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}{x}_{k}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{k}{x}_{j}+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j}+2{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}+({\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j} \\\\  & =({A}^{T}x)_{k}+(Ax)_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all $1\\leq k \\leq n$, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A,$ $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T  \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209954 #edb/ref 1065 13115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209954 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "332e4bab-6a1d-5868-a616-4a2c034c2619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186209954 #edb/ref 1067 "The Gradient of a Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539475 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186540149 #edb/r