$L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47594296-da7d-5903-b008-c5f5265a8817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552433 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 34\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}62 \\\\ 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-269476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9669b6bb-a27d-584f-935a-b35adacb11c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552476 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}52 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5918743-e3c0-5f81-afbc-29edc571e474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552489 #edb/ref 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-3 \\\\ -3+0+2 \\\\ -3+0+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-276050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1ab528e-b16d-505b-916d-2fee71d7c43b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552502 #edb/ref 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 \\\\ 1+0+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552540 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552540 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d52e92a-c60f-53ce-b6fa-3a5a9d253411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552540 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ad729a2-27a9-5de3-9be0-9c031aba9da4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552585 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552585 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96a34648-e1ed-5f17-886a-1b36c08e876b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552585 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186208956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552597 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552597 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb0a810a-de11-5a10-9c67-4ea3706facd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552489 #edb/ref 1759218604541